{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310627,"nonce":"HGOztnATtoQbFOToF4ka1p8GI2s=","title":"Passing `new Class()` directly as an interface PARAM to an instance method silently dropped the class→iface fat-pointer wrap → later ...","message":"FIXED in hir_to_mir.rs direct-symbol-resolution path (the `is_user_defined \u0026\u0026 !receiver_needs_special_dispatch` branch). Previously called `args.iter().filter_map(|a| self.lower_expression(a))` — bypassing `maybe_materialize_for_call`. Now for `*is_method` calls, each non-receiver arg is materialized at the call boundary, picking up Path 3 (class→interface fat-pointer wrap).\n\nWhy this layer matters: the other method-call paths in hir_to_mir.rs (e.g. the `is_method \u0026\u0026 !treat_as_static_call` branch around line 16548 and the virtual-dispatch path around line 11716) already called `maybe_materialize_for_call`. The direct-symbol-resolution path was the odd one out, fast-path for user-defined methods. With `(c:Container).setG(new Hi())` where `Container.setG(x:Greet)` stores `x` in an iface field, the fast path dropped the wrap, the iface field got a raw class ptr, and later `c.g.hello()` SIGSEGV'd reading garbage as a vtable.\n\nHow to apply: when adding/refactoring method-call lowering in hir_to_mir.rs, ALL paths that pass user args to a callee must route through `maybe_materialize_for_call` (or the equivalent Path 3 wrap). Skipping it on the fast path is the documented footgun.\n\nRepro that surfaced this: nue's `ArchRegistry.register(\"llama\", new LlamaArch())` — the iface param case. Llama-chat hit SIGSEGV in `arch.validate(...)` because `arch:Builder` was a raw `LlamaArch` ptr, not a fat ptr.\n\nRelated: [[bugs-stdlib-mir-wrapper-import-clash]] surfaced after this fix because llama-chat could now reach the LM head builder which references `arr.length`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_iface_arg_to_instance_method` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}